{"fingerprint":"cbcd8239d6ae4f14e898570870c5c8a2307be219241f0bd15141862c39f40b9b","path":"src/review_gauntlet/checkpoint.py","rule_id":"data-validation","content":"In `write_latest_checkpoint`, the pointer file is written via `pointer_tmp.write_text(...)` then `pointer_tmp.replace(pointer_path)`. However, the old pointer backup uses `pointer_path.rename(pointer_backup_dir)` which moves the pointer into a directory path—this only works if `pointer_path.is_dir()` (line 338). When the pointer is a regular file (the normal case), the old pointer is not backed up before replacement, so a crash between `replace` and cleanup could leave the pointer in a half-updated state without recovery. The `is_dir()` guard on line 338 suggests this was intended for a different scenario but doesn't protect the regular-file case.","suggestion_code":"","existing_code":"        pointer_tmp.write_text(checkpoint_id + "\n", encoding="utf-8")
        if pointer_path.is_dir():
            pointer_path.rename(pointer_backup_dir)
        pointer_tmp.replace(pointer_path)","start_line":337,"end_line":340,"thinking":"The `replace` call is atomic on POSIX for file-to-file, so the regular-file path is actually safe on POSIX. But the `is_dir()` branch is there to handle a corrupted state where the pointer became a directory. The real issue is that if pointer_path is a directory, renaming it to pointer_backup_dir and then replacing with a file is not atomic—a crash between rename and replace leaves no pointer at all. But this is an edge-case recovery path, not the normal flow.","imprecise":false}

